<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for a signed release. A release signed with a Developer ID is
  notarised only with the hardened runtime on, and that runtime kills a process that maps
  executable memory without an entitlement. tauri applies this one file to every executable it
  signs in the bundle: the app, and the three externalBin files.

  EXACTLY TWO KEYS, and both are for the bundled Node (`flywheel-node`, nodejs.org's binary), which
  runs the sidecar:
    * allow-jit: V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code in pages it maps with MAP_JIT. Without this
      key the Node process is killed at start, and the sidecar never runs.
    * allow-unsigned-executable-memory: V8 also writes and executes code in pages that are not MAP_JIT.
  Nothing else is granted:
    * no disable-library-validation: the sidecar loads no native add-on, and every library in the bundle
      carries the same team's signature.
    * no microphone, camera, location or network entitlement: the app is not sandboxed, and an entitlement
      that grants a capability a user then approves in a dialog is not needed here.
  The app itself, which runs no JavaScript of its own (WKWebView does, in Apple's process), gets the two keys
  too. tauri has one file for all of them. If a later bundle step signs the Node alone, the app drops them.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
